  "findings": [
    {
      "description": "Transfer is not atomic: the balance check and the two UPDATE statements are executed outside a transaction. Concurrent transfers can read stale balances and result in double-spending, lost updates, or overdrawn accounts. This is a critical data-integrity issue in a money-movement path.",
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "description": "The destination account existence is never verified before updating its balance. A transfer to a nonexistent dst_id silently succeeds while the source account is debited, causing loss of funds.",
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "description": "No validation of amount: negative values are not rejected, so a caller could pass a negative amount and effectively move funds in the reverse direction or inflate balances.",
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "description": "The function returns SQL errors and other failures implicitly without handling them; no rollback or error propagation is defined, so partial updates can leave accounts inconsistent.",
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    }
  ],
  "decision": "REJECT",
  "fix_description": "The transfer function must be made atomic and safe: (1) wrap all balance check and both UPDATE statements in a single database transaction with COMMIT on success and ROLLBACK on any error, so concurrent transfers cannot cause double-spending or lost updates; (2) verify that dst_id exists before debiting src_id, returning 404/error if not found; (3) validate amount is a positive finite number and reject zero/negative/invalid values before any balance mutation; (4) catch database errors, roll back on failure, and propagate/return a clear error instead of leaving partial updates. get_balance should also handle missing rows safely. These fixes address the critical atomicity issue, the high-severity missing destination and amount-validation issues, and the medium-severity error-handling gap.",
